1
00:00:01,395 --> 00:00:08,605
Welcome to Asia episode five of seven. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:27,128
Our seven South Asian stops are Pakistan, India, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh,
Sri Lanka and the Maldives. A labelled supplemental map will bring the
Maldives’ tiny islands into view.

3
00:00:27,795 --> 00:00:31,336
Let's bring Pakistan and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:34,744 --> 00:00:46,551
Pakistan lies west of India and southeast of Afghanistan, with an Arabian Sea
coast in the south. The Indus River runs through much of its length.

5
00:00:47,215 --> 00:01:00,137
Mohenjo-daro preserves evidence of carefully planned city life in the ancient
Indus civilisation. Its organised streets show that urban planning is
thousands of years old.

6
00:01:00,795 --> 00:01:12,010
Picture the Indus leading south to the sea, with an ancient planned city
beside it. Pakistan is the western coastal anchor of our South Asian group.

7
00:01:12,852 --> 00:01:17,798
Let's bring India and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:37,443
India fills most of the South Asian peninsula, between the Arabian Sea and Bay
of Bengal. Pakistan is west, the Himalayan countries north, and Bangladesh
near its eastern side.

9
00:01:38,103 --> 00:01:50,468
The Taj Mahal at Agra is a mausoleum, commissioned by Shah Jahan in memory of
his wife. Its famous white marble belongs to a tomb complex, rather than a
royal residence.

10
00:01:51,136 --> 00:02:03,466
Remember the broad peninsula pointing into the Indian Ocean. Place the marble
mausoleum in northern India, and keep the surrounding smaller countries in
their separate positions.

11
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,567
Let's bring Nepal and its neighbours into view.

12
00:02:11,614 --> 00:02:24,153
Nepal is the long, narrow inland country along the Himalayas, between India
and China. Bhutan lies farther east, separated from Nepal by Indian territory.

13
00:02:24,804 --> 00:02:41,004
Nepal is not only high peaks: Chitwan National Park protects lowland habitat
for one-horned rhinoceroses and Bengal tigers. The country rises from warm
plains to Himalayan mountains.

14
00:02:41,659 --> 00:02:51,980
Picture a rhinoceros below the mountains. Nepal is the western member of the
Nepal-Bhutan pair, stretched along India’s northern edge.

15
00:02:54,945 --> 00:03:06,160
Bhutan is the smaller Himalayan country east of Nepal, between India and
China. It is landlocked, above northeastern India and north of Bangladesh.

16
00:03:06,815 --> 00:03:19,435
The Drametse mask dance combines drumming with dancers wearing animal and
mythical masks. This sacred performance is a living Bhutanese tradition passed
between generations.

17
00:03:20,089 --> 00:03:30,329
Remember masked dancers in the eastern Himalayan pocket. Nepal is the long
strip farther west; Bhutan is the compact country to the east.

18
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:35,459
Let's bring Bangladesh and its neighbours into view.

19
00:03:38,546 --> 00:03:50,620
Bangladesh sits on the Bay of Bengal, almost surrounded by India, with Myanmar
along part of its southeastern border. Great rivers spread across its low
delta.

20
00:03:51,282 --> 00:04:02,114
The Sundarbans contain salt-tolerant mangrove forests threaded by tidal
channels. Bengal tigers live in this watery landscape shared across the
Bangladesh-India border.

21
00:04:02,765 --> 00:04:14,108
Picture a tiger among mangroves at the river delta. Bangladesh belongs at the
head of the Bay of Bengal, below the Himalayan countries and east of most of
India.

22
00:04:14,796 --> 00:04:18,418
Let's bring Sri Lanka and its neighbours into view.

23
00:04:23,103 --> 00:04:32,019
Sri Lanka is the island immediately southeast of India’s southern tip. A
narrow stretch of water separates the two countries.

24
00:04:32,675 --> 00:04:44,320
Sigiriya’s Lion Rock rises steeply above the surrounding landscape, with the
remains of a royal capital on its slopes and summit. A monumental lion once
framed the approach.

25
00:04:44,984 --> 00:04:56,536
Picture the lion rock on the island below India. Keep Sri Lanka close to
India’s southeastern tip; the Maldives are the much smaller islands farther
southwest.

26
00:04:57,450 --> 00:05:02,907
Let's find Maldives on a labelled supplemental map, then move in for a closer
view.

27
00:05:09,743 --> 00:05:22,793
The Maldives form a chain of small coral islands in the Indian Ocean,
southwest of India and Sri Lanka. This supplemental view shows real land
polygons; blue gaps are sea and lagoons.

28
00:05:23,447 --> 00:05:37,309
Baa Atoll is a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve with coral reefs, islands and rich
marine life. The country’s story is spread across the sea, rather than
contained in one large island.

29
00:05:37,974 --> 00:05:49,781
Remember a north-south necklace of coral islands southwest of Sri Lanka. These
are close views of tiny land areas, not one solid strip of territory.

30
00:05:55,983 --> 00:06:12,003
Pakistan anchors the west; India fills the broad peninsula. Nepal and Bhutan
sit along the Himalayan side, Bangladesh at the river delta, Sri Lanka below
India’s southeastern tip, and the Maldives farther southwest.

31
00:06:12,655 --> 00:06:32,095
Remember the planned Indus city, marble mausoleum, lowland rhinoceros, masked
dance, mangrove tiger, lion rock and coral islands. For the Maldives, we zoom
into real tiny land areas rather than enlarging the country’s territory.
